WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.019
We put a master swordsman

00:00:02.019 --> 00:00:04.354
in front of a table full of blades.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:07.733
This is what a character might do
with these weapons.

00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:10.354
This is Meldof’s hammer.

00:00:10.354 --> 00:00:11.385
 Yes  

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.325
Welcome back to a special episode of Punch
4 Punch.

00:00:16.658 --> 00:00:19.745
You guys at home
and Netflix seem to like us. So?

00:00:19.745 --> 00:00:20.078
We’re back!

00:00:20.078 --> 00:00:22.706
And we're back to talk about Blood Origin,
The New Witcher series coming up.

00:00:22.706 --> 00:00:25.000
It has cool sword fights
and axe fights and dagger fights.

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:25.834
We want to figure out

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:29.046
what's the magic sauce to make the stuff
look good on screen.

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:32.966
So we invited our friend Luke La Fontaine,
expert swordsman choreographer,

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:33.550
to join us.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:38.263
And we put a master swordsman
in front of a table full of blades.

00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:40.932
There's some weapons here that are similar
to the weapons in the show.

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:43.310
Why don't we get right to it,
pick them up and start using them?

00:00:43.685 --> 00:00:44.728
Well, actually,

00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:47.773
before we get right to it, I know
this is a special Punch 4 Punch episode.

00:00:48.065 --> 00:00:50.275
But you got to start
off with the right tone.

00:00:51.943 --> 00:00:54.404
Hit em with it!

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:00.410
Hey, what's up, guys?

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.578
So we are going to be taking a look

00:01:01.578 --> 00:01:04.247
at some of the fight scenes
here from Blood, Origin and Luke.

00:01:04.498 --> 00:01:06.667
You know everything
there is to know about swords, right?

00:01:07.084 --> 00:01:08.585
Maybe, yeah.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.462
Especially when it comes like filming it.

00:01:10.462 --> 00:01:12.547
So I want to get your break down
on all this.

00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:13.924
Absolutely. I can't wait to watch it.

00:01:13.924 --> 00:01:15.926
Luke real quick,
tell us a little about yourself.

00:01:15.926 --> 00:01:19.012
Ever since I was about seven years old,
I was obsessed with swords.

00:01:19.054 --> 00:01:21.431
It took me to Japan, it took me to Europe.

00:01:21.473 --> 00:01:24.393
I was fortunate enough to study
with a number of different masters.

00:01:24.643 --> 00:01:28.689
16 years old, I did my first film,
which was the original Karate Kid.

00:01:28.814 --> 00:01:31.400
That was my first experience
into the world of stunts.

00:01:31.441 --> 00:01:34.236
I decided I was going to come out
to California and become a stunt man.

00:01:34.277 --> 00:01:38.115
And here we are, 39 years later to enable
me to be here with you guys today.

00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:38.907
And, you know,

00:01:38.907 --> 00:01:42.744
I always cherish being able to talk about
what I love with good friends.

00:01:42.744 --> 00:01:43.662
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:43.662 --> 00:01:45.831
Well, let's jump into a scene.

00:01:45.831 --> 00:01:47.332
Fight as a clan.

00:01:47.332 --> 00:01:51.962
We may just make it out

00:01:51.962 --> 00:01:55.465
alive.

00:01:55.465 --> 00:01:59.511
Right off the bat.

00:01:59.511 --> 00:02:01.179
I love what they're doing with this.

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:04.850
It makes brilliant sense
in terms of the people

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.061
with the larger weapons and the shields
are at the front of the line defending

00:02:08.353 --> 00:02:13.108
and the elf that's got the doubled smaller
daggers is behind doing cleanup.

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:16.570
This is very, very realistic combat.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.529
And I like it.

00:02:17.529 --> 00:02:21.074
They're actually literally tactically
doing things that would make sense.

00:02:21.324 --> 00:02:23.201
They're throwing spears, daggers.

00:02:23.201 --> 00:02:25.746
The sword strikes are for vulnerable
parts.

00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:28.665
There was a guy on top of that table,
Michelle Yeoh cut his legs out, boom.

00:02:28.665 --> 00:02:30.041
He hit that guy in the neck.

00:02:30.041 --> 00:02:33.462
Why? There's a gap in the neck, right
where the axe would be able to go.

00:02:33.587 --> 00:02:35.881
They also start with sword and shield.

00:02:35.881 --> 00:02:38.300
They don't end with sword and shield.
Exactly.

00:02:38.383 --> 00:02:41.553
There's an evolution to the characters
and it just adds more

00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:43.346
drama
and makes us care about these characters.

00:02:43.346 --> 00:02:46.183
The story of the fight goes
somewhere, right?

00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:48.643
And the audience gets
to go there with you, which is awesome.

00:02:48.894 --> 00:02:50.562
You've gotten rusty.

00:02:50.562 --> 00:02:53.982
Have I?

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.405
Fights like this are so much harder to do.

00:03:00.405 --> 00:03:01.239
Oh, yeah.

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:04.451
You're dealing with so many elements
at the same time.

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:07.329
How do I make this look believable
between three people?

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:10.081
And everybody
is getting the right amount of time

00:03:10.081 --> 00:03:11.833
so that somebody is not left out.

00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:13.752
I have to admit,
I would have been intimidated

00:03:13.752 --> 00:03:15.879
and gone “uhh”
scratching my head going, okay,

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:17.798
Michelle,
I'm going to need your help on this a lot.

00:03:17.798 --> 00:03:18.131
Right.

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:21.009
Out of the three of them,
she is probably the most skilled,

00:03:21.343 --> 00:03:26.097
not only as an actress performer, but
as the character that she's portraying.

00:03:26.264 --> 00:03:28.767
So therefore, it shows
that she's constantly controlling,

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.103
still moving everybody around her
and keeping everybody safe

00:03:32.354 --> 00:03:34.481
while still proving her dominance
over them.

00:03:34.731 --> 00:03:38.652
She's
using their hot headedness against them.

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:42.614
And what's great
is that's being very clearly told.

00:03:42.614 --> 00:03:44.825
You got to love when like somebody does.

00:03:44.825 --> 00:03:47.869
The typical master thing like,
I'mma beat you with a sheath or twig

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:54.042
against your blades.

00:03:54.042 --> 00:03:57.712
That’s a bold choice to start with,

00:03:58.713 --> 00:04:01.299
boom again, really smart.

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:02.676
Look at the armor design.

00:04:02.676 --> 00:04:04.427
Look at the placement of her blades.

00:04:04.427 --> 00:04:06.429
She's cutting people
where the armor isn't.

00:04:06.429 --> 00:04:08.014
She's moving.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:09.850
Yeah, she's not staying stationary.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.519
She's getting out of the way
of these characters.

00:04:12.853 --> 00:04:15.605
Getting a lot of the actress herself
actually doing moves too.

00:04:15.814 --> 00:04:20.694
It's great using doubles when you can,
but like you need an element of the actor

00:04:20.694 --> 00:04:23.947
being physical and getting in there
just to earn the character.

00:04:23.947 --> 00:04:29.035
Every time you see an actor or an actress
put that work in, it's besides it

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:32.622
being believable in the storyline,
you wind up respecting

00:04:32.622 --> 00:04:36.418
and defending that actor because you know
that actor put that extra work in.

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:37.752
Oh, look at that guy, huh?

00:04:37.752 --> 00:04:40.839
You did some flourishes Okay. Yeah.

00:04:40.839 --> 00:04:43.383
What did we do? Somebody’s
been paying attention.

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:45.927
What did we do in To The Death
and what do we see?

00:04:46.177 --> 00:04:47.929
I'm going to do a bunch of sword
flourishes.

00:04:47.929 --> 00:04:53.101
I'm going to wind up paying for it
because he wasn't having it.

00:04:53.101 --> 00:04:55.645
So we looked at all the choreography,
the tactics, techniques here,

00:04:55.645 --> 00:04:57.522
but we didn't talk about the weapons
themselves.

00:04:57.522 --> 00:05:01.318
Luckily, we have the master armor,
Nick Jeffries, to teach all of us

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:04.487
exactly what was his process
in making these amazing weapons

00:05:04.487 --> 00:05:06.615
that we see
being swung around in this amazing show?

00:05:06.865 --> 00:05:09.951
Let's go talk to him
and learn about why he made the decision.

00:05:09.951 --> 00:05:14.497
Oh, yeah, I'm dying to see that.

00:05:14.497 --> 00:05:16.625
So we are on the line with Nick Jeffries
here.

00:05:16.625 --> 00:05:17.918
Nick, thanks for joining us.

00:05:17.918 --> 00:05:19.836
Could you tell us a little bit
about yourself?

00:05:19.836 --> 00:05:21.463
Yeah, sure. My name's Nick Jeffries.

00:05:21.463 --> 00:05:26.009
And I'm the master armor
for The Witcher and its spinoffs.

00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:27.218
What a cool job title.

00:05:28.511 --> 00:05:31.056
Can you tell me a little bit
about what you did on Blood Origin here?

00:05:31.056 --> 00:05:33.183
Did you make the weapons?

00:05:33.183 --> 00:05:34.684
Did you help conceptualize them?

00:05:34.684 --> 00:05:36.603
Every stage is mine.

00:05:36.603 --> 00:05:39.981
I read the script, write down
who needs the weapon, and then we come up

00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:41.066
with the finished item.

00:05:41.066 --> 00:05:42.859
We have the hero weapon that's real.

00:05:42.859 --> 00:05:47.280
So if it’s stationary, lying on the table,
holding it.

00:05:47.322 --> 00:05:49.658
If it's really close up,
we use the real one.

00:05:49.658 --> 00:05:52.661
In all other situations we use rubber.

00:05:52.661 --> 00:05:56.998
What was the inspiration behind the weapon
designs that you did for Blood Origin?

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:02.462
What I did with Blood Origin was push back
into more ancient swords from Bronze

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:07.425
Age weapons, but we have used steel
because they have that technology.

00:06:07.509 --> 00:06:12.180
This is actually the Soul Reaver,
the Holy Weapon that the quest is for.

00:06:12.222 --> 00:06:16.935
This actually has a bronze core in it,
but it has a steel outter.

00:06:17.018 --> 00:06:22.148
But the bronze is a direct connection
with the bronze in the blade Any body

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:25.986
you kill, you have a direct connection
in the bronze to them.

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:26.653
I love that.

00:06:26.653 --> 00:06:29.072
That's cool. Yeah, that's super cool.
All that worldbuilding.

00:06:29.072 --> 00:06:31.574
Wow. That's a sick axe.

00:06:31.574 --> 00:06:34.494
Yeah, that's Fjall’s big axe. Now that,

00:06:35.996 --> 00:06:37.872
as you can probably hear, is hollow.

00:06:37.872 --> 00:06:40.667
It doesn't look like it's hollow,
but it's made out three mil plate.

00:06:41.084 --> 00:06:43.461
If we made this solid, you can imagine.

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:46.506
It would be like 14 pounds.

00:06:46.673 --> 00:06:47.841
Plus being steel.

00:06:47.841 --> 00:06:50.343
It lets me get some proper
looking age to it.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:53.638
I would like to claim this stuff.

00:06:53.888 --> 00:06:57.058
It's not in a museum
because nobody's found it yet.

00:06:57.642 --> 00:07:00.812
But if we made it really rusty
and put it in a hole in the ground.

00:07:00.812 --> 00:07:03.398
They’d go “Oh, fantastic!”

00:07:04.649 --> 00:07:06.276
What I want to show you next actually

00:07:06.276 --> 00:07:10.613
is the only dedicated Dwarven weapon.

00:07:10.613 --> 00:07:14.534
So this, is Meldorf’s hammer.

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:19.289
It's called Gwen,
which is Meldorf’s murdered girlfriend.

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:21.249
She talks to this.

00:07:21.249 --> 00:07:24.961
These things
here, that actually says GWEN on the side.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.339
So we've made it like they’re
both screw caps

00:07:28.631 --> 00:07:32.552
and it's hollow inside
and it contains the ashes.

00:07:32.886 --> 00:07:34.345
Oh, wow.

00:07:34.345 --> 00:07:37.307
Everything is perfect in my mind for her.

00:07:37.307 --> 00:07:40.143
Perfect person playing the perfect role.

00:07:40.143 --> 00:07:42.812
And then we managed
to give them the perfect weapon.

00:07:42.812 --> 00:07:45.940
So I'm really
happy with how that came out.

00:07:46.983 --> 00:07:47.776
All the different weapons you

00:07:47.776 --> 00:07:50.403
have in there from Blood Origin,
if you were in that world

00:07:50.779 --> 00:07:54.074
and you had to go into battle,
which weapon would you take with you?

00:07:55.492 --> 00:07:59.370
I like the daggers that were Éile’s
because they have this ring on the end.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:07:59.954
Yeah.

00:07:59.954 --> 00:08:02.999
And once you have a ring,
you can do so much more with that.

00:08:03.166 --> 00:08:07.462
If you can use something for real
it would probably be this dagger,

00:08:07.462 --> 00:08:08.838
of everything we made.

00:08:08.838 --> 00:08:11.382
Well well, Nick, thank you.
Thank you so much for taking the time.

00:08:11.382 --> 00:08:13.343
Like every time you would step away

00:08:13.343 --> 00:08:15.887
and bring out another blade, we’d
always be like “Yes, yes, yes”.

00:08:16.721 --> 00:08:18.014
That's so true.

00:08:18.014 --> 00:08:21.434
See, this is the thing about armory
and the way we do it, really.

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:24.854
You've just had a little insight
into our unusual world.

00:08:25.063 --> 00:08:26.189
I love it. Yeah.

00:08:26.189 --> 00:08:28.066
Thank you. Yeah, that's amazing.

00:08:28.066 --> 00:08:29.901
You're welcome. It's a pleasure.
All right.

00:08:29.901 --> 00:08:31.653
We learned about these weapons.

00:08:31.653 --> 00:08:34.697
Now, Luke, will you show us
how to make them look good on camera?

00:08:34.948 --> 00:08:36.491
Absolutely will, let's go have some fun.

00:08:41.371 --> 00:08:43.123
Luke, on this table in front of you.

00:08:43.123 --> 00:08:44.332
I have three weapons,

00:08:44.332 --> 00:08:46.543
weapons that are close approximations
to what are in the show

00:08:46.543 --> 00:08:48.086
because the actual weapons are often
anglon.

00:08:48.086 --> 00:08:49.546
So we've got the closest thing we could,

00:08:49.546 --> 00:08:52.966
but I was hoping you could show us
how to make these look cool on camera.

00:08:53.049 --> 00:08:54.676
Let's unveil the first pair.

00:08:54.676 --> 00:08:56.511
under the red veil.

00:08:56.511 --> 00:08:58.721
We have daggers.

00:08:58.721 --> 00:08:59.305
Not a problem.

00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:00.723
Boom. Here we go.

00:09:00.723 --> 00:09:02.433
We've got a pair.

00:09:02.433 --> 00:09:05.061
Not exactly matched of Kukris.

00:09:05.061 --> 00:09:07.564
If we're talking about practicality
in terms of combat,

00:09:07.564 --> 00:09:10.441
both forward is the most practical way
to use these weapons,

00:09:10.692 --> 00:09:14.654
but it's a fantasy show, so screw that,
we aren’t doing that.

00:09:14.946 --> 00:09:16.322
My cutting patterns.

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:19.868
X’s, Horizontal cuts, upward cuts.

00:09:20.368 --> 00:09:24.664
If I make a combination where I cut down,
I cut across, I cut up, I cut down,

00:09:24.831 --> 00:09:27.917
I can come up with any kind of pattern
I want to come through here

00:09:27.917 --> 00:09:30.044
and cut somebody. And stab.

00:09:30.712 --> 00:09:33.006
There's no limit on how they can be used.

00:09:33.381 --> 00:09:35.258
In terms of a fantasy piece.

00:09:35.258 --> 00:09:37.385
I might be able to demo
a little bit further

00:09:37.677 --> 00:09:40.638
if I have another weapon
for these to go against.

00:09:40.847 --> 00:09:43.683
Luckily, under the yellow cloth we have...

00:09:44.559 --> 00:09:46.853
broadsword. Is this called a broadsword?

00:09:46.853 --> 00:09:51.941
Yeah, technically, that is a Chinese Dao
or Chinese broadsword.

00:09:51.983 --> 00:09:55.361
This is very, very typically
a military weapon in ancient China.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:10:00.200
And for the show it's very similar
to Michelle Yeoh's character sword.

00:10:00.325 --> 00:10:02.869
You notice that's a steel sword.

00:10:02.911 --> 00:10:06.789
Yeah, it's a Wushu practice sword,
which means it's not sharp,

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:10.293
but it's still steel
and it's got a thin edge.

00:10:10.543 --> 00:10:13.087
So we're going to be careful
in how we use it.

00:10:13.213 --> 00:10:16.507
Here in the States, we're a little bit
more on that cowboy edge.

00:10:16.507 --> 00:10:19.344
We will mix materials where in England
they won't.

00:10:19.344 --> 00:10:21.512
But I believe as part of the Declaration
of Independence, it said...

00:10:21.763 --> 00:10:24.390
“But we also want to be able to use
metal weapons in our martial arts scene.”

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:25.558
So yeah, right, right.

00:10:25.558 --> 00:10:29.020
And that's written in there somewhere,
you know, it absolutely is.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:30.521
So we have a dance partner here for Luke

00:10:30.521 --> 00:10:32.690
because Gui and I we're
just going to be watching and talking.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:34.859
So Stephen Dunford, if you can join us,

00:10:34.859 --> 00:10:37.612
you guys have watched the To the Death
short film on the Corridor Channel.

00:10:37.779 --> 00:10:39.948
Steve is one of the guys
with the energy swords

00:10:40.031 --> 00:10:43.368
And that's why he's here today
to put his hands on that sword,

00:10:43.493 --> 00:10:47.538
because I trust Stephen Dunford
not to split my head open.

00:10:47.538 --> 00:10:51.376
Okay, so this is what a character might do
with these weapons.

00:10:51.542 --> 00:10:53.878
He cuts vertically at my head, bang.

00:10:53.878 --> 00:10:56.714
Now I'm doing what's called an X block.

00:10:56.714 --> 00:11:00.885
When I oppose with both weapons,
I'm scissoring the blade in between

00:11:00.885 --> 00:11:02.011
both blades.

00:11:02.011 --> 00:11:04.597
So there's nowhere for the blade
to slide off to.

00:11:04.806 --> 00:11:08.434
I want to take advantage of this
and use one weapon to attack

00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:09.811
and one weapon to defend.

00:11:09.811 --> 00:11:15.441
So I use my right hand to slide the weapon
off out of my trajectory

00:11:15.817 --> 00:11:19.404
and make a cut on him where he's
going to take a diagonal cut down at me

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.865
and we both miss
he makes a horizontal cut.

00:11:23.866 --> 00:11:25.743
I can parry double

00:11:25.743 --> 00:11:29.455
which puts a wall of steel in between me
and the other blade.

00:11:29.539 --> 00:11:33.835
I can also do the exact same x block
if I choose to

00:11:34.085 --> 00:11:41.009
and deliver a fatal blow.

00:11:41.009 --> 00:11:43.052
Okay, so you showed us
some cool moves with daggers.

00:11:43.094 --> 00:11:44.387
So now we're looking at this blade here.

00:11:44.387 --> 00:11:44.554
You know,

00:11:44.554 --> 00:11:46.597
this is basically the equivalent
of the sword that Michelle Yeoh

00:11:46.597 --> 00:11:48.308
is using in Blood Origin.

00:11:48.308 --> 00:11:51.853
How would you amp this up and make it
stylistically look good on camera?

00:11:51.936 --> 00:11:55.064
If I was going to draw this weapon,
I want to present the sword.

00:11:55.273 --> 00:11:57.483
Once I draw the sword,

00:11:57.483 --> 00:12:00.945
the sword wraps around my body.

00:12:01.029 --> 00:12:04.240
Chinese
Daos are about keeping the sword moving,

00:12:04.324 --> 00:12:07.702
mainly because in a lot of instances,
military combat.

00:12:07.702 --> 00:12:09.579
You're not fighting one on one.

00:12:09.579 --> 00:12:11.414
You're in a battle
with hundreds of people.

00:12:11.414 --> 00:12:14.709
If I keep the sword tight to myself,
I can parry

00:12:14.917 --> 00:12:19.505
and keep myself wrapped in steel
while I stab and cut other people.

00:12:19.505 --> 00:12:23.843
It's a light, agile weapon
